  "summary": "Dr. Elena Martinez, a 34-year-old quantum physicist from Barcelona, led a team at Stanford University that discovered Adaptive Quantum Stabilization (AQS), a quantum error correction method that reduces error rates by 87.3%. The breakthrough occurred on May 23, 2023, after 18 months of research funded by $5.15 million from NSF, DOE, and TechCorp Industries. The method improved quantum coherence time by 8.2x and enabled scaling to 256 qubits. Dr. Martinez received the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2024 for this work, which is expected to accelerate practical quantum computing by 5-7 years."
